                    "a": "Discuss with students the way a historian studies a site. The greatest amount of information can be determined from careful excavation. When a site is excavated, photograph or draw soil changes and artifacts as these are uncovered. After artifacts are removed from the ground, they are labeled with information that shows where they were found. These steps assure that a historian can tell the context of artifacts (where they are found, and how the artifacts related to each other).",
                    "q": "Discuss with students the way a historian studies a site. The greatest amount of information can be determined from careful excavation. When a site is excavated, photograph or draw soil changes and artifacts as these are uncovered. After artifacts are removed from the ground, they are labeled with information that shows where they were found. These steps assure that a historian can tell the context of artifacts (where they are found, and how the artifacts related to each other)."
