            "exercises": [
              {
                "items": [
                  {
                    "a": "Change in the speed and direction of light rays due to change of medium.",
                    "q": "What is refraction of light?"
                  },
                  {
                    "a": "When light travels from a rare medium (e.g. air) into a denser medium (e.g. water or glass), it bends towards the normal.",
                    "q": "When does light bend towards the normal?"
                  },
                  {
                    "a": "When light travels from a denser medium (e.g. water) into a rare medium (e.g. air), it bends away from the normal.",
                    "q": "When does light bend away from the normal?"
                  },
                  {
                    "a": "It changes its speed, but not its direction.",
                    "q": "What happens when a light ray falls perpendicular to the surface of a denser medium?"
                  },
                  {
                    "a": "An imaginary line drawn perpendicular to the surface of the medium (such as glass or water).",
                    "q": "What is the normal?"
                  },
                  {
                    "a": "The angle between the incident ray and the normal.",
                    "q": "What is the angle of incidence?"
                  },
                  {
                    "a": "The angle between the refracted ray and the normal.",
                    "q": "What is the angle of refraction?"
                  },
                  {
                    "a": "Because of refraction — when light passes from water to air it changes speed and direction, so the pencil appears bent at the water surface.",
                    "q": "Why does a pencil in water look broken or bent?"
                  }
                ],
                "label": "Refraction of Light"
              },
              {
                "items": [
                  {
                    "a": "The angle of incidence for which the angle of refraction is 90°. It is denoted by 'c'.",
                    "q": "What is the critical angle?"
                  },
                  {
                    "a": "Water 48.8°, crown glass 41.1°, diamond 24.4° (Table 9.3).",
                    "q": "What is the critical angle of water, crown glass and diamond?"
                  },
                  {
                    "a": "When the angle of incidence is greater than the critical angle, the light rays reflect back into the same denser medium.",
                    "q": "What is total internal reflection?"
                  },
                  {
                    "a": "(i) Light rays travel from a denser medium to a rarer medium. (ii) The angle of incidence is greater than the critical angle.",
                    "q": "What are the two conditions for total internal reflection?"
                  },
                  {
                    "a": "An optical instrument that works on the principle of total internal reflection. It has two mirrors fitted at 45° at each end of a tube, facing each other, so light from a hidden object is reflected to the observer.",
                    "q": "What is a periscope?"
                  },
                  {
                    "a": "An illusion of water in deserts and on roads, caused by refraction of light through non-uniform (hot and cool) layers of air followed by total internal reflection.",
                    "q": "What is a mirage?"
                  },
                  {
                    "a": "Fish under water see a reflected view of the outside world (sun, moon, plants, birds) because light reaching the water surface at more than the critical angle is reflected back into the water by total internal reflection.",
                    "q": "What is the fish eye view?"
                  }
                ],
                "label": "Critical Angle and Total Internal Reflection"
              },
              {
                "items": [
                  {
                    "a": "The splitting of white light into its constituent colours (components).",
                    "q": "What is dispersion of light?"
                  },
                  {
                    "a": "Red, orange, yellow, green, blue, indigo and violet.",
                    "q": "Name the seven colours of the spectrum."
                  },
                  {
                    "a": "The band of seven colours (red, orange, yellow, green, blue, indigo and violet) that appears on the dispersion of white light.",
                    "q": "What is a spectrum?"
                  },
                  {
                    "a": "The best natural example of the spectrum of white light — the multi-coloured arch that appears in the sky after rain in the monsoon season.",
                    "q": "What is a rainbow?"
                  },
                  {
                    "a": "Red, green and blue — the three colours of light that produce white light when combined with the correct intensity.",
                    "q": "What are primary colours of light?"
                  },
                  {
                    "a": "By the addition of primary colours in equal intensities.",
                    "q": "How are secondary colours formed?"
                  },
                  {
                    "a": "Because it reflects red light and absorbs all the other colours of white light. An object appears in the colour it reflects; it appears white if it reflects all colours and black if it absorbs all colours.",
                    "q": "Why does a rose look red?"
                  }
                ],
                "label": "Dispersion, Spectrum and Colours"
              }
            ],
